  "id": "M32/p1",
  "lesson": "M32",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>How many riffle shuffles until a 52-card deck is closer to random than not, meaning that the total variation distance from the uniform distribution over all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>52</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">52!</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">52</span><span class=\"mclose\">!</span></span></span></span> orders falls below one half?</p>",
  "answer": "7",
  "answerKind": "numeric",
  "tol": 1,
  "options": [],
  "explain": "<p>Seven. Bayer and Diaconis computed the distance exactly: after 5 shuffles it is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.924</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.924</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.924</span></span></span></span>, after 6 <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.614</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.614</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.614</span></span></span></span>, after 7 <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.334</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.334</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.334</span></span></span></span>, after 8 <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.167</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.167</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.167</span></span></span></span>. For the first four shuffles it is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.000</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.000</span></span></span></span> to three places, and then it collapses in a few steps. You will reproduce the table in e05.</p>"
 },
